					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=2005044&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606401735" alt="Decibelles" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=2005044)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/decibelles" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(2005044); return false;" >Decibelles</a></span> <span class="odate time_1552270840 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">11 Mar 2019 02:20</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4180544">
									

<p>Your plan to work with expunging a description by having the Foundation throw personnel at it until they can figure out how to contain it isn't a bad idea, and your concept for how the SCP functions is potentially interesting, but in the end the article as a whole feels flimsy.</p>
<p>It's not flimsy from a logical standpoint (as far as I can tell), but a large chunk of what happens following the description is set on detailing a specific and otherwise par-for-the-course series of events in a short timeframe. As a result, it counter-intuitively makes the SCP <em>less</em> interesting, because you try to define it through grounded means. Recovery logs are somewhat of a staple, and it comes off as routine when all the logs effectively do is tell me how the Foundation contain it. I know they also serve to inform the reader of what's going on with the SCP, but that's not the feeling I get when I finish the article.</p>
<p>That said, you know what you're doing because you know why expunged descriptions work and fail, and that you work with your article to make up for the lack of a description. That it doesn't inherently collapse is props to you as a writer. But with articles like Swann's Proposal and 3005 showcasing the narrative angle of expunging a description, and articles like 3942 and 579 showcasing a more clinical and logic puzzle-esque angle, I'm able to see instances of description expunging working in different scenarios. This doesn't successfully land in either angle, so I have to downvote.</p>

							</div>

								
